      confidence: 0.91

  - id: pl_surtsey
    as_written: "Surtsey"
    resolved:
      name: "Surtsey, Iceland"
      point: [-20.60, 63.30]
      bounds: [[-20.65, 63.28], [-20.55, 63.32]]
      kind: natural
      provider: nominatim
      resolved_at: "2026-08-20T00:00:00Z"
      confidence: 0.89

# ─── AUDIO AND MEDIA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty, and present only because the schema lists both as required. This
# story has no recordings and no images by design - its narration is text and
# whoever plays it speaks it. Requiring these to exist forces every text-only
# document to carry two empty lists to be considered valid, which is a defect
# in the schema rather than anything about this story.

audio: []
media: []

# ─── ANNOTATIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

annotations:
  - id: ann_surtsey_reserve
    kind: circle
    geometry: { type: Point, coordinates: [-20.60, 63.30] }
    radius_m: 6000
    label: "Closed to all but research"
    style:
      color: "#ff9d00"
      fill_color: "#ff9d00"
      fill_opacity: 0.18
      weight: 2

# ─── SCENES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Narration is text. Nothing here names a recording, because there is not one
# and there does not need to be.

scenes:
  - id: sc_01_ridge
    sequence: 1
    title: "The Ridge"
    place_id: pl_iceland
    basemap: bm_dark
    transition: cut
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 11 }
    content:
      caption: "Iceland sits astride the Mid-Atlantic Ridge."
      narration: >-
        Iceland is the only place on Earth where the Mid-Atlantic Ridge rises
        above the sea. The island straddles the boundary between the North
        American and Eurasian plates, which are pulling apart beneath it at
        roughly two centimetres a year. The country is not finished. It is
        still being made.

  - id: sc_02_thingvellir
    sequence: 2
    title: "Where the Plates Part"
    place_id: pl_thingvellir
    basemap: bm_satellite
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 12 }
    content:
      caption: "Þingvellir — the rift you can walk into."
      narration: >-
        At Þingvellir you can walk down into the rift itself. The valley floor
        has dropped as the plates draw apart, leaving a wall of basalt on
        either side. Icelanders gathered here from the year 930 to hold the
        Alþingi, one of the oldest parliaments in the world, in a place that
        was quietly tearing open beneath them.

  - id: sc_03_reykjavik
    sequence: 3
    title: "A City on the Heat"
    place_id: pl_reykjavik
    basemap: bm_dark
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 10 }
    content:
      caption: "Reykjavík runs on the heat below it."
      narration: >-
        Reykjavík takes its name from the steam its first settlers saw rising
        from the ground. Today almost every building in the city is heated by
        water drawn hot from the rock beneath, and nearly all of the country's
        electricity comes from geothermal and hydroelectric power.

  - id: sc_04_krafla
    sequence: 4
    title: "The Krafla Fires"
    place_id: pl_krafla
    basemap: bm_terrain
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 11 }
    content:
      caption: "Krafla, 1975 to 1984 — nine years of rifting."
      narration: >-
        In the north, the Krafla volcanic system spent nine years coming apart.
        Between 1975 and 1984 the ground rose and fell repeatedly as magma
        moved sideways underground, splitting the crust in a slow series of
        eruptions and quakes that geologists watched from the beginning.

  - id: sc_05_vatnajokull
    sequence: 5
    title: "Ice Over Fire"
    place_id: pl_vatnajokull
    basemap: bm_satellite
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 10 }
    content:
      caption: "Vatnajökull — Europe's largest ice cap by volume."
      narration: >-
        Vatnajökull covers about eight percent of the country, and beneath it
        sit several active volcanoes. Ice and magma are an unstable pairing:
        when one erupts under the other, the meltwater has nowhere to go but
        out.

  - id: sc_06_grimsvotn
    sequence: 6
    title: "The Glacier Flood"
    place_id: pl_grimsvotn
    basemap: bm_satellite
    transition: dissolve
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 11 }
    content:
      caption: "Grímsvötn — meltwater held, then released."
      narration: >-
        Grímsvötn lies buried under the ice, melting a lake above itself that
        drains catastrophically when the pressure gives way. Icelandic has a
        word for the resulting flood: jökulhlaup. In 1996 one of them carried
        away a stretch of the ring road along the south coast.

  - id: sc_07_eyjafjallajokull
    sequence: 7
    title: "The Ash Cloud"
    place_id: pl_eyjafjallajokull
    basemap: bm_terrain
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 12 }
    content:
      caption: "Eyjafjallajökull, 2010 — a small eruption, a large disruption."
      narration: >-
        In April 2010, meltwater met rising magma under Eyjafjallajökull and
        shattered it into fine ash. The eruption was modest by Icelandic
        standards, but the wind carried the ash straight into European
        airspace, and for several days most flights across the continent were
        grounded.

  - id: sc_08_heimaey
    sequence: 8
    title: "Cooling the Lava"
    place_id: pl_heimaey
    basemap: bm_satellite
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 12 }
    content:
      caption: "Heimaey, 1973 — a harbour saved with seawater."
      narration: >-
        On Heimaey in 1973 a fissure opened along the edge of the town
        overnight, and the population was evacuated by fishing boat before
        dawn. As lava advanced on the harbour, crews pumped seawater onto its
        front for months to chill and slow it. The harbour survived, and the
        new rock left it better sheltered than before.

  - id: sc_09_surtsey
    sequence: 9
    title: "An Island From Nothing"
    place_id: pl_surtsey
    basemap: bm_satellite
    transition: fly
    annotation_ids: [ann_surtsey_reserve]
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 12 }
    content:
      caption: "Surtsey — born 1963, and left alone ever since."
      narration: >-
        Ten years earlier and a little to the south, a fishing crew had watched
        smoke rise from open water. The eruption lasted until 1967 and left
        behind an island where there had been none. Surtsey has been closed to
        almost everyone since the day it appeared, so that biologists can watch
        what arrives, in what order, entirely undisturbed.

  - id: sc_10_unfinished
    sequence: 10
    title: "Unfinished"
    place_id: pl_iceland
    basemap: bm_dark
    transition: fly
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 10 }
    content:
      caption: "Still being built."
      narration: >-
        None of this is history. The plates are still separating, the ice still
        sits on the fire, and new land still arrives without much notice.
        Iceland is a country you can watch being built.

attribution:
  - "© OpenStreetMap contributors © CARTO"
  - "© OpenStreetMap contributors, SRTM | © OpenTopoMap (CC-BY-SA)"
  - "© Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics"
